The ideal solution would be Abstract classes! the idea is that those classes can yield an instance so if we make:
public abstract class Animal {...}
Animal animal = new Animal(...); //Compilation error

we can use this idea to define abstract methods - which means we define methods without implementation every non abstract class that inherits from an abstract class
must implement all abstract method. Thus we force our sub classes to implement certain methods. (else the code won't compile)

More on abstract classes - They can inherit from other abstract classes in the case an abstract class inherits from other abstract it doesn't have to
implement the methods of the abstract class (although it can). Abstract classes hold anything like regular classes you just can't create an instance of them.
we will use these classes when it will make no sense to create a general object. Or when we want to force an API on a group of inheriting classes but the parent
class cannot provide a reasonable implementation for this API.